## Is AI the Right Tool?

**AI can process thousands of CVs in seconds. But should it decide who deserves a job interview?**

When I think about the impact of AI on women and bias, recruitment is one area where I believe AI is not always the right tool. Companies can use AI to screen CVs, rank candidates and identify people who appear to be the best match for a position. This can save recruiters time, especially when they receive thousands of applications.

However, faster does not necessarily mean better or fairer.

Imagine a woman applying for a management position after taking two years away from work. Her CV shows a career gap, but it does not explain why. She may have taken time away to care for children, support a family member, study, deal with personal circumstances or simply take a different career path.

An AI system might see the gap as a negative factor because it has been trained using historical recruitment data. If previous hiring decisions favoured candidates with continuous career histories, the system could learn that pattern and consider candidates with career gaps less suitable.

This creates a problem because the AI is not actually understanding the person behind the CV. It is identifying patterns in data.

This is where I think **“no AI” can sometimes be the better choice**.

A recruiter can look at the same CV and recognise that a career gap does not automatically mean that someone is less qualified. They can consider the candidate's skills, experience, motivation and the context behind their career history. They can also ask questions during an interview instead of making a judgement based only on patterns in previous data.

There is also a difference between using AI to **support** a recruiter and allowing AI to **make the decision**. I would be much more comfortable with AI helping organise applications or carrying out repetitive administrative tasks than deciding which candidates should be rejected.

The cost of making the wrong decision is also much higher here than simply being slower. A company might save time by automatically rejecting certain candidates, but it could also lose talented people and reinforce existing inequalities.

For me, the issue is not that AI is always biased or that companies should stop using it. The problem is using AI in situations where **human context and judgement are essential**.

Sometimes the most responsible question is not *“How can we use AI for this?”* but **“Do we actually need AI for this at all?”**

That is why, when it comes to important recruitment decisions that can affect someone's career, I believe **human judgement should remain at the centre.**
